;;; **W36's second witness (`W36/alt-flag-two-derivations`): the alternatives
;;; flag moves `refuted by` beside an unmoved core, in every order.**
;;;
;;; M1f S1f.5.15, filed on the coordinator's follow-up of 2026-11-08 to the
;;; user's ruling on Q-M1f.5 (a), *"Register row W36"*: one row per witness,
;;; `open` and owned by F22. Written during the stage's T3 to find a program
;;; that no rule order hides, which the first witness,
;;; `alt-flag-refuted-by.ein`, is not: a derived order printed its right
;;; answer there.
;;;
;;; ── The shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(u a)` is derived twice: by `u-long` from three givens, `(h1 a) (h2 a)
;;; (h3 a)`, and by `u-short` from two, `(g1 a) (g2 a)`. `u-long` is written
;;; first, and the two are one kind of rule, a join concluding a positive, so
;;; every order puts it first: FIFO, the written and arrival order, and any
;;; order derived from a rule's shape alike. `k1` concludes `(false)` from
;;; `(u a)`, and `v` and `w` derive `(q a)` and `(not (q a))` from the same two
;;; givens `u-short` reads, a pair contradiction.
;;;
;;; With every derivation recorded, `(false)`'s smallest frontier is
;;; `(g1 a) (g2 a)` through `u-short`, the pair's is the same, and the core
;;; search takes the core over `(false)`: `refuted by k1`. With the flag off a
;;; fact keeps its first derivation alone, `(u a)`'s is `u-long`'s, `(false)`
;;; rests on three facts, and the core is taken over the pair: `refuted by v,
;;; w`. The core is `(g1 a) (g2 a)` either way.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine says ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   ein solve FILE, at a3cb1753 (2026-11-08), release:
;;;     as written, the flag off      unsat core (g1 a) (g2 a) · refuted by v, w
;;;     the config line taken out     unsat core (g1 a) (g2 a) · refuted by k1
;;;
;;; R is empty and exhausted either way, and `(solve :expect (false))` holds.
;;; Under the order T3 derived from each rule's shape (never shipped) it said
;;; the same (`build/s1f.5.15/w36b/`, 2026-11-08).
;;;
;;; ── Why it is wrong ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; As `alt-flag-refuted-by.ein`'s header says: the flag's exemption is the
;;; core (Q-M1e2.11 (ii)), `refuted by` follows the core only where it moved
;;; (Q-M1e2.21 (a)), and here it did not, so `refuted by` is the default's,
;;; `k1`. F22 re-derives what the store does not keep when the search reaches
;;; it, which makes `refuted by` a function of the core.

(relation g1 T :why "{?1} has g1")
(relation g2 T :why "{?1} has g2")
(relation h1 T :why "{?1} has h1")
(relation h2 T :why "{?1} has h2")
(relation h3 T :why "{?1} has h3")
(relation u  T :why "{?1} is used")
(relation q  T :why "{?1} is a q")
(g1 a) (g2 a) (h1 a) (h2 a) (h3 a)
(rule u-long ()
  :match  (and (h1 ?x) (h2 ?x) (h3 ?x))
  :assert (u ?x)
  :why    "{?x} has all three h")
(rule u-short ()
  :match  (and (g1 ?x) (g2 ?x))
  :assert (u ?x)
  :why    "{?x} has both g")
(rule k1 ()
  :match  (u ?x)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} is used")
(rule v ()
  :match  (and (g1 ?x) (g2 ?x))
  :assert (q ?x)
  :why    "{?x} is a q")
(rule w ()
  :match  (and (g2 ?x) (g1 ?x))
  :assert (not (q ?x))
  :why    "{?x} is not a q")

(solve :expect (false))

;; The flag the row is about: off, a fact keeps its first derivation alone.
(config :record-alternative-justifications false)
